      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to account for the fact that the digits may repeat. The formula for the number of distinct permutations of a multiset is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this problem:\n- \\( n = 4 \\) (since we have four digits: 2, 2, 9, 9)\n- The digit 2 appears 2 times\n- The digit 9 appears 2 times\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the factorials:\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
